Subject: EPIS: Sensory evidence 

In <9309150254.AA06347@snark.lehman.com>, "Perry E. Metzger" writes:
|> davisd@pierce.ee.washington.edu says:
[...]
|> People might overinterpret that way. English phrases can possess
|> multiple interpretations --  some advocate the use of E-prime to
|> encourage enough precision of expression that such ambiguities cease
|> to be a problem. (I tried to phrase that in E-Prime -- perhaps someone
|> can say it better?)

You have made proper use of E', but I find the claim too strong. GS
accepts the fuzziness of words and tries to work with it, reminding us
that the people communicating supply whatever meaning is derived,
based on their reactions to the words. Using E' removes sombunall
ambiguity, but if you really want disambiguated English you'll have to
carry a good chunk of the world along and tag each phrase with its
extended context in space-time; and even then you'd have to avoid or
explain puns and specify which meaning of each significant word you
had in mind. In short, you'd have to become an enormous bore. The
previous sentence makes this paragraph self-referential.

|> As for the particular question of how someone might draw the inference
|> I stated -- the overgeneralization might be 
|> 
|> 1) sensory data is always valid
|> 2) I don't sense a bus
|> 3) Therefore, there is no bus about to hit me
|> 
|> Of course the correct chain is
|> 
|> 1) sensory data appears to be valid most of the time
|> 2) I don't sense a bus
|> 3) Therefore, from what I can tell thus far, no bus is about to
|>    hit me.
|> 

2) I didn't sense a bus when I checked the street 2[1] sec. ago, and
experience to date suggests that busses do not travel fast enough or
off-road such that one could appear in that time;

2a) My best current estimate of my reaction/evasion time is slightly
over 2 seconds;

3) Therefore, I infer at this time that no bus is about to hit me. 

Etc.

Someone willing to backtrack more of his early experience and training
could add more assumptions and inferences (more or less justified), ad
nauseam. I see this process as interesting (if tedious) both because
we may find and remove unrealistic assumptions in our own makeup, and
because all of them must be accounted for in the design of an
autonomous AI.

