Date: Wed, 15 Sep 1993 12:05:37 -0400 (EDT)
From: Mark Sulkowski <SULKOM@ubvmsb.cc.buffalo.edu>
Subject: EPIS: Sensory Evidence

 From: "Perry E. Metzger" <pmetzger@lehman.com>
>davisd@pierce.ee.washington.edu says:
>> I ask, however, how does "sensory evidence is ALWAYS valid" mean "that
>> which I do not model does not exist"?
>
>As for the particular question of how someone might draw the inference
>I stated -- the overgeneralization might be 
>
>1) sensory data is always valid
>2) I don't sense a bus
>3) Therefore, there is no bus about to hit me
>
>Of course the correct chain is
>
>1) sensory data appears to be valid most of the time
>2) I don't sense a bus
>3) Therefore, from what I can tell thus far, no bus is about to
>   hit me.

	Maybe the problem is that you are understanding "valid" to imply
omniscience.  It isn't intended that way.  Most people won't read it that
way.  (Though your chain is correct GIVEN your particular spin on the word.)

	Of course, the best way to understand her statement is to read
the damn books that explain it.  :)  That way we won't have to rewrite
all of her stuff in E-Prime.

	Though it might improve _Atlas Shrugged_.  Hmmm, there's a thought. :)


>> Further, just who do you think was making the claim that "that which
>> I do not model does not exist"?
>
>Ayn Rand, for practical purposes, seems to have operated on much that
>rule of thumb, yes.

	To be fair, she would not support that statement.  But, yes, at
times she denied the existence (or truth rather) of whatever didn't jibe
with her models.


