Date: Wed, 15 Sep 1993 11:11:33 -0500
From: extr@jido.b30.ingr.com (Freeman Craig Presson)
Subject: Inventions & concepts

In <ggZeKdq00iV2ECDLp_@andrew.cmu.edu>, Christina M Grimes writes:
[...]
|> I accept your point, to a certain degree. However, I think there is a
|> distinction we both missed: concepts and rights are neither discoveries
|> nor inventions, but in between, comparable to spontaneous orders.

This was covered -- albeit circuitously.

[...]
|> Concepts are "developed": they are created, like inventions, but are
|> objective, like discoveries. Concepts do not exist unless there are
|> intelligent beings to have and understand concepts. At the same time,
|> concepts are not arbitrary--they depend entirely on reality and
|> objectivity.

Good point -- but another way of saying that there exist objects and
pragmas that are !I and !D.

|> Also, I see no reason to conclude that invention can't go on without
|> concepts. What about the monkey in those experiments that piles boxes
|> and stands on them in order to reach a banana suspended from the
|> ceiling? I would think it had invented a kind of stool. It certainly has
|> no idea of what a "stool" is, or what a thing that elevates is, but it
|> just stacked those boxes to have something higher to stand on. Even
|> though this is sticky ground (we need to know just what's an invention
|> and what's a tool), I just can't think of any better example right now.
|> {:)

Well, let's back up. Those experiments were with chimps (_Pan
Troglodytes_) and not monkeys, and I'm not sure your assertion about
what ideas a chimp may have is well-supported. In fact, I'd be willing
to grant provisionally that the chimp may not form a concept like
"stool" (manufactured object for sitting or stepping) but he may very
well be said to know what he can climb on and what he can't, to know
that some things are taller than others, and that the "stacking"
operation makes things-he-can-climb into taller-things. That's pretty
concept-ish.

